---
name: setup-writing-style
description: Learn a person's writing style from samples and turn it into a reusable style guide, so future drafts sound like them. Use when the user wants Claude to write "like me", match their voice, set up a personal or brand writing style, analyze writing samples, or fix drafts that sound generic or AI-written. Produces a style profile they can store in their preferences or reuse as instructions.
---

# Setting up a writing style

## 1. Collect samples

Ask for **3–6 pieces** of the user's own writing (emails, posts, reports, messages), ideally of different contexts (casual and formal), 150+ words each. Say what to avoid: text heavily edited by others, text written by AI, quotes. Also ask:

- Who do you write for, and in which situations will Claude draft for you?
- Language(s) and regional variant.
- Anything you dislike in AI-written text? Words, structures, tone.
- Is there a writer or publication whose tone you aim for?

## 2. Analyze (privately, then summarize)

Examine each dimension and note concrete evidence:

| Dimension | What to look for |
|---|---|
| Voice and register | Formal/casual; warm/dry/direct; humor; use of "I" and "we"; tu/vous or equivalent |
| Sentence rhythm | Average length; mix of short and long; fragments; questions |
| Paragraphs | Length, openers, transitions, how arguments are built |
| Vocabulary | Plain vs. technical; favorite words and phrases; words they never use; jargon level |
| Structure habits | Opens with context or with the point; uses lists, headers, bold; sign-offs |
| Punctuation and mechanics | Dashes, parentheses, exclamation marks, ellipses, emoji, capitalization, contractions, numerals, date formats |
| Stance | Hedging vs. assertiveness; how they ask, refuse, apologize, thank |
| Quirks | Repeated moves that make the text recognizably theirs |

Base findings on **patterns across samples**, not one-off choices. Quote short evidence for each finding (a few words).

## 3. Write the style profile

A compact guide (300–600 words) Claude can follow, in this shape:

```
STYLE PROFILE — <name or brand>
Overview: <2 sentences describing the voice>
Do:
- …(specific, observable behaviors)
Don't:
- …(including banned phrases and structures)
Rhythm and structure: …
Vocabulary: prefers … ; avoids …
Mechanics: …
Context adjustments: emails … / social posts … / formal documents …
Mini-example (original lines written in this style, not copied from the samples)
```

Prefer specific rules ("opens with the request in the first sentence; sentences average 14 words; no exclamation marks") over adjectives ("friendly and professional").

## 4. Test and calibrate

1. Draft two short pieces (different contexts) on topics the user chooses, using only the profile.
2. Ask the user to mark what sounds right or wrong, line by line if possible.
3. Revise the profile; re-test. Usually two rounds suffice.
4. Ask whether any rule should differ by context.

## 5. Deliver and use

- Give the final profile in a block the user can copy into their preferences or a project's instructions. Offer a short version (under 100 words) for limited fields.
- When later drafting in this style: follow the profile, keep the user's facts and opinions, and **do not imitate someone else's voice** or claim their authorship in a deceptive way.
- Ghostwriting for the user's own communications is fine; the user remains responsible for what they send.

## Cautions

- Samples are private: use them only for this purpose and do not quote them back at length.
- Do not infer sensitive traits from writing.
- Avoid caricature: capture habits, not exaggerations.
- A style evolves; offer to update the profile after new samples or feedback.
